WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.200
-Liam, welcome back to the show.

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.634
I'm always happy
when you come on the show.

00:00:03.634 --> 00:00:05.000
-It's great to see you again.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.667
-I will say, every time
I do see you outside the show,

00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:09.100
it's usually at
Madison Square Garden.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.601
We're at a Knick game or
a Ranger game.

00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:13.334
And, you know,
they put the celebrities,

00:00:13.334 --> 00:00:15.033
every now and then,
on the jumbotron.

00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:16.434
-Yeah.
-And I --

00:00:16.434 --> 00:00:18.100
-What's it called? Jumbotron?

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.501
-The jumbotron, yeah.
It's the big screen there, yeah.

00:00:20.501 --> 00:00:23.033
And they -- I never --
'Cause they throw me up there

00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:24.501
every now and then.

00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:27.968
But I don't like following you,
because everyone just loves you.

00:00:27.968 --> 00:00:29.400
Yeah, they go, "Liam Neeson."

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.000
They show a clip
from one of your movies.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.634
Then they cut to you
and you just look like a stud,

00:00:32.634 --> 00:00:34.834
and everyone goes, "Yeah!"
and they freak out.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:36.200
And then I feel like
I've got to top you when they

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:37.334
put me on the jumbotron.

00:00:37.334 --> 00:00:38.567
I'm, like,
pouring beer on myself.

00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:41.100
I'm, like, doing, like,
you know, wacky stuff.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.400
Don't ever follow Liam Neeson
on a jumbotron.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.267
That's my only advice
to everyone.

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:46.400
I'm happy you're here.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.734
I'm happy that you're here
for Perm Week.

00:00:48.734 --> 00:00:50.868
It's a big, exciting thing
in New York City.

00:00:50.868 --> 00:00:53.000
Everyone's talking about it.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.434
I asked you backstage, very far
away from you when I asked,

00:00:57.434 --> 00:00:59.133
have you ever had a perm?

00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:02.434
-You know, it's one of those
words I grew up with

00:01:02.434 --> 00:01:04.934
hearing my mother say
and my sister say.

00:01:04.934 --> 00:01:07.434
I never quite knew what it was.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:09.300
It's like
a permanent wave, yeah.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.534
-It's a curl, right?
-A curl, yeah.

00:01:11.534 --> 00:01:13.133
But you had long hair
at one point.

00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:16.200
Like, a -- Yeah.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.501
You brought us a photo.

00:01:17.501 --> 00:01:19.467
I don't know if anyone's
ever seen you with long hair.

00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:20.667
Look at this stud.

00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:22.167
What's his story?

00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:23.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:26.467
This looks like he's in
Pink Floyd or something, right?

00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:28.100
-Dave Gilmour.
-You look like Dave Gilmour.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.467
-A little bit.
-You do. Yeah.

00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:32.167
What was he all about?
-19-- Sorry, buddy.

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.734
1972. I was 20.

00:01:34.734 --> 00:01:37.334
Tara, long since dead.
-Aww.

00:01:37.334 --> 00:01:38.868
-"Ban the bomb."

00:01:38.868 --> 00:01:40.634
-Oh, yeah,
you had a peace symbol.

00:01:40.634 --> 00:01:43.000
Were you a hippie?

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.968
-Loons.
You don't remember Loons, Jimmy.

00:01:44.968 --> 00:01:47.100
Before your time. Big flared.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.601
-Oh, yeah. Bell-bottoms?

00:01:48.601 --> 00:01:49.701
-Yeah.
-Oh, yeah, of course.

00:01:49.701 --> 00:01:51.968
Yeah, yeah, of course.

00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:54.100
Barefoot, as well.
-Oh, barefoot, too, yeah.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.734
-Yeah, this was, like,
taken in, like, Woodstock. Yeah.

00:01:57.734 --> 00:02:01.667
Did you keep this phase
for a while?

00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:04.167
-I did. It's funny enough.

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:08.868
This was just after I met
my brother Ciarán Hinds.

00:02:08.868 --> 00:02:10.334
I regard him as my brother.

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:11.801
-Yeah.
-Brilliant actor.

00:02:11.801 --> 00:02:14.267
And we were
at a theater festival

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:18.901
in a place called Enschede --
30 miles south of Amsterdam.

00:02:18.901 --> 00:02:22.033
First time ever on an airplane.
You know...

00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:23.434
-Exciting.
-All that stuff. Oh, my God.

00:02:23.434 --> 00:02:24.501
-And you landed
your first acting gig

00:02:24.501 --> 00:02:26.434
not too far after this.

00:02:26.434 --> 00:02:32.367
-Uh...yeah, 1976, yeah,
I started professionally.

00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:34.067
-But can you tell everyone
how you got cast?

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:36.267
'Cause I think it's pretty...

00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:39.334
-Okay, there's a theater
that Ciarán and myself

00:02:39.334 --> 00:02:43.601
are patrons of in Belfast
called the Lyric Theatre.

00:02:43.601 --> 00:02:45.167
Very, very good theater.

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:49.734
And I was working
in an architect's office,

00:02:49.734 --> 00:02:51.234
doing prints and stuff.

00:02:51.234 --> 00:02:53.767
Kept saying to these guys,
"I'm going to be an actor.

00:02:53.767 --> 00:02:55.467
I'm going to be
a professional actor."

00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:57.834
And one of them called
my bluff on lunchtime.

00:02:57.834 --> 00:02:59.534
He said,
"Call up the Lyric Theatre."

00:02:59.534 --> 00:03:01.434
They literally pushed
the telephone --

00:03:01.434 --> 00:03:03.834
It was one of the old ones,
you know?

00:03:03.834 --> 00:03:08.601
And I called, and the lady
who formed the theater,

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:11.334
called Mary O'Malley --
she picked it up,

00:03:11.334 --> 00:03:14.100
and I started vomiting,
"I want to be an actor.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.534
I've done all
these amateur plays.

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:17.567
I want to be a professional,"
and stuff.

00:03:17.567 --> 00:03:21.400
There was a pause, and then she
said, "What height are you?"

00:03:22.934 --> 00:03:24.968
And I said, 6'4."

00:03:24.968 --> 00:03:28.367
She said,
"Be up here next Thursday."

00:03:28.367 --> 00:03:31.033
And that was kind of it,
you know?

00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:32.367
[ Cheers and applause ]
-That's how you got cast.

00:03:32.367 --> 00:03:33.868
-Yeah.
-6'4."

00:03:33.868 --> 00:03:35.400
They were looking
for someone that tall?

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:37.601
-Exactly. Yeah, yeah.

00:03:37.601 --> 00:03:39.133
-Your height got you the gig.
-The height got me the gig.

00:03:39.133 --> 00:03:40.367
-I love it.

00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:42.734
And now your new movie,
"Marlowe," by the way,

00:03:42.734 --> 00:03:44.434
it's your 100th movie.

00:03:44.434 --> 00:03:46.934
-100th. Yeah, yeah.
-100 movies.

00:03:46.934 --> 00:03:49.000
[ Cheers and applause ]
100 movies.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.367
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:50.367 --> 00:03:53.367
You know, you've played
so many different characters.

00:03:53.367 --> 00:03:55.834
Do you know when fans
recognize you on the street,

00:03:55.834 --> 00:03:58.200
what movie they recognize
you from immediately?

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.534
-You know, I have a funny story.

00:04:00.534 --> 00:04:02.868
I hope it's funny.
And I'll tell it quick.

00:04:02.868 --> 00:04:05.567
My wife and I were walking
through the Plaza Athénée --

00:04:05.567 --> 00:04:09.100
really, really beautiful hotel
on the Right Bank in Paris.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:10.434
-Yeah.

00:04:10.434 --> 00:04:13.534
-And there's tables
on either side of this corridor

00:04:13.534 --> 00:04:15.567
leading to the foyer.

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:18.067
And people sitting, having a
glass or wine or cup of tea,

00:04:18.067 --> 00:04:19.501
sandwiches, and stuff.

00:04:19.501 --> 00:04:22.968
And as we passed, there was
two sets of elderly couples --

00:04:22.968 --> 00:04:25.100
two ladies
and two older gentlemen.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.434
And as we passed, one of
the ladies stood up and said,

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:29.634
"Excuse me."

00:04:29.634 --> 00:04:32.033
I said, "Yep, sorry."

00:04:32.033 --> 00:04:36.701
"My husbands are going out for
the Normandy Day landings,

00:04:36.701 --> 00:04:38.000
the 50th anniversary."

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.100
-Wow.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.400
-And I was like, "Oh, my gosh,"

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.400
and, you know, shook their hands
and thanked them.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.534
"Thank you for your service,"
all that stuff, you know?

00:04:45.534 --> 00:04:47.701
It was a little bit
embarrassing, but, my God.

00:04:47.701 --> 00:04:52.434
And they hadn't been to Normandy
since the landings, you know?

00:04:52.434 --> 00:04:53.634
-Oof!

00:04:53.634 --> 00:04:58.234
-And the Plaza Athénée
has these beautiful menus

00:04:58.234 --> 00:05:00.534
with a gold thread
going through it, you know?

00:05:00.534 --> 00:05:01.868
-Oh, fancy.

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:04.200
-And she says, "Would you ever
give us an autograph?"

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.334
I said, "I'd be honored."
-Yeah.

00:05:06.334 --> 00:05:08.701
-So she passed me this,
and I have a pen.

00:05:08.701 --> 00:05:10.200
And as I'm about to write,
she says,

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.000
"We loved
'The English Patient.'"

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.234
So I signed it "Ralph Fiennes."

00:05:17.234 --> 00:05:19.234
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:19.234 --> 00:05:21.868
[ Applause ]

00:05:21.868 --> 00:05:24.067
Why ruin it? Why ruin it?
-No.

00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:26.868
-They're having a great day when
they met Ralph Fiennes.

00:05:26.868 --> 00:05:28.534
-Yeah, they met Ralph Fiennes.

00:05:28.534 --> 00:05:31.133
-Does Ralph know that you --
-Oh, yeah, I've told him that.

00:05:31.133 --> 00:05:32.534
-Yeah.
-He's a good pal.

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:33.968
-He's a good --
I love that you let them go --

00:05:33.968 --> 00:05:35.133
"Yeah, why not?

00:05:35.133 --> 00:05:37.834
Yeah, Ralph Fiennes. Yeah."
That's fantastic.

00:05:37.834 --> 00:05:39.300
Let's talk about
"Marlowe," okay?

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:42.601
This is a noir crime thriller
set in the 1930s Los Angeles.

00:05:42.601 --> 00:05:44.000
-Yeah, '38, '39.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.434
-And you play a detective,
which, I think, great casting.

00:05:47.434 --> 00:05:49.601
-Private eye.
-A private eye, yeah.

00:05:49.601 --> 00:05:52.834
-Jessica Lange, Diane Kruger.
-Oh, yeah.

00:05:52.834 --> 00:05:55.534
-We had a great, great --
Alan Cumming.

00:05:55.534 --> 00:05:56.901
Brilliant -- Alan Cumming.

00:05:56.901 --> 00:05:59.367
-Do you like playing a detective
or, like, set in the '30s?

00:05:59.367 --> 00:06:00.601
Is that fun for you?

00:06:00.601 --> 00:06:02.601
-Yeah, especially when
you wear the trilby hat.

00:06:02.601 --> 00:06:05.567
You know, that just seals it.
-Yeah, you look great in that.

00:06:05.567 --> 00:06:08.067
I want to show a clip.
Here's Liam Neeson in "Marlowe."

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:10.133
Take a look at this.

00:06:10.133 --> 00:06:13.367
-Thank you.
